items.statement:
Let $(I,A,D,P,P^{\vee},q)$ be a symmetrizable Cartan datum ([[def-symmetrizable-cartan-datum-for-a-quantum-group]], [[def-generalized-cartan-matrix]]), let $M$ be a chosen $P$-graded type-1 quantum weight module with $E_i$ and $F_i$ locally nilpotent ([[def-quantum-weight-module-and-integrability]]), and fix $i\in I$. For $\mu\in P$ put
$$H_\mu:=\ker(E_i)\cap M_\mu,\qquad a_\mu:=\langle\mu,h_i\rangle .$$
Then:

(i) $H_\mu=0$ whenever $a_\mu<0$;

(ii) if $a_\mu\ge0$, then $F_i^{(n)}:H_\mu\to M_{\mu-n\alpha_i}$ is injective for $0\le n\le a_\mu$ and is the zero map for $n>a_\mu$;

(iii) every $u\in M_\lambda$ has a unique finite **admissible decomposition** $u=\sum_{n\ge0}F_i^{(n)}u_n$ with $u_n\in H_{\lambda+n\alpha_i}$ and $u_n=0$ whenever $n>\langle\lambda+n\alpha_i,h_i\rangle$;

(iv) consequently $M_\lambda$ is the internal direct sum of the nonzero admissible string spaces $F_i^{(n)}H_{\lambda+n\alpha_i}$.

Coefficients beyond a string's bottom are specified to be zero; arbitrary killed coefficients at the top are not allowed. No finite-dimensionality of $M$ or of its weight spaces, no bounded support, no uniform nilpotence and no choice principle is assumed; $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ is the integer pairing of the datum ([[def-kac-moody-integral-and-dominant-integral-weights]]) and $F_i^{(n)}$ is the divided power of [[def-quantum-integers-factorials-and-divided-powers-at-q-i]].

Let $A_0$ be the local ring of [[def-the-local-ring-at-q-zero]] and let $M$ be an integrable weight module with its Kashiwara operators $\tilde e_i,\tilde f_i$ ([[def-kashiwara-crystal-operators]], [[def-quantum-weight-module-and-integrability]]).

A **crystal lattice** of $M$ is a free $A_0$-submodule $L\subseteq M$ such that

(i) $L$ generates $M$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$, i.e. the canonical map $\mathbb Q(q)\otimes_{A_0}L\to M$ is an isomorphism;

(ii) $L=\bigoplus_{\mu\in P}L_\mu$ with $L_\mu:=L\cap M_\mu$, and each $L_\mu$ is again a free $A_0$-module;

(iii) $\tilde e_iL\subseteq L$ and $\tilde f_iL\subseteq L$ for all $i\in I$.

Because $A_0$ is a principal ideal domain, each graded piece $L_\mu$ has a well-defined rank $\operatorname{rank}_{A_0}L_\mu$, and (ii) says that $\operatorname{rank}_{A_0}L$ is the sum of the ranks of the $L_\mu$.

A **crystal basis** of $M$ is a pair $(L,B)$ consisting of a crystal lattice $L$ and a $\mathbb Q$-basis $B$ of the crystal limit $L/qL\cong\mathbb Q\otimes_{A_0}L$ ([[def-the-local-ring-at-q-zero]]) such that

(iv) $B=\bigsqcup_{\mu\in P}B_\mu$, where $B_\mu:=B\cap(L_\mu/qL_\mu)$;

(v) $\tilde e_iB\subseteq B\cup\{0\}$ and $\tilde f_iB\subseteq B\cup\{0\}$ for all $i\in I$;

(vi) for all $b,b'\in B$ and all $i$: $\tilde f_ib=b'$ if and only if $b=\tilde e_ib'$; here $\tilde e_i,\tilde f_i$ denote the operators induced on $L/qL$.

The **crystal graph** of $(L,B)$ is the coloured directed graph with vertex set $B$ and an arrow $b\xrightarrow{i}b'$ whenever $\tilde f_ib=b'$ (by (vi) this is equivalent to $b=\tilde e_ib'$). The **crystal of $M$** is this graph, together with the weight function $b\mapsto\mu$ for $b\in B_\mu$. For every $\mu\in P$ one has $\dim_{\mathbb Q(q)}M_\mu=\operatorname{rank}_{A_0}L_\mu=\#B_\mu$.

A **crystal basis of the negative half** $U_q^-$ is defined by the same data with $U_q^-$ in place of $M$: a free $A_0$-submodule $L\subseteq U_q^-$ with $U_q^-\cong\mathbb Q(q)\otimes_{A_0}L$ and $\tilde e_iL,\tilde f_iL\subseteq L$ ([[thm-the-i-string-decomposition-of-the-negative-half]]), together with a $\mathbb Q$-basis $B$ of $L/qL$ satisfying (v) and (vi) for the operators on the negative half. Here the root grading replaces the weight grading, and the lattice and residue basis are required to respect that root grading. In the negative half, $\tilde e_i\tilde f_i=\mathrm{id}$ implies $\tilde f_iB\subseteq B$; this refinement is specific to its infinite strings. No choice principle is used in these definitions.

Let $(L_1,B_1),(L_2,B_2)$ be crystal bases of integrable modules as in [[thm-crystal-basis-tensor-product-rule]] and let $B_1\otimes B_2$ be the tensor product crystal of [[def-tensor-product-of-crystals]] with the two-case operators of that theorem. Then for all $b_1\in B_1$, $b_2\in B_2$ and $i\in I$:

(a) $wt(b_1\otimes b_2)=wt(b_1)+wt(b_2)$;

(b) $\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\max\bigl(\varepsilon_i(b_1),\ \varepsilon_i(b_2)-\langle h_i,wt(b_1)\rangle\bigr)$;

(c) $\varphi_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\max\bigl(\varphi_i(b_1)+\langle h_i,wt(b_2)\rangle,\ \varphi_i(b_2)\bigr)$;

(d) the two cases are exactly the alternatives in the max formulas: $\tilde f_i$ acts on the first factor if and only if $\varphi_i(b_1)>\varepsilon_i(b_2)$, and then $\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\varepsilon_i(b_1)$ (this equality also holds at the tie); it acts on the second factor if and only if $\varphi_i(b_1)\le\varepsilon_i(b_2)$, and then $\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\varepsilon_i(b_2)-\langle h_i,wt(b_1)\rangle$, and dually $\tilde e_i$ acts on the first factor if and only if $\varphi_i(b_1)\ge\varepsilon_i(b_2)$;

(e) **partial-inverse property.** For all $b,b'\in B_1\otimes B_2$ and every $i$, $\tilde f_ib=b'$ if and only if $b=\tilde e_ib'$; in particular the assignment of the non-strict comparison to $\tilde e_i$ and the strict comparison to $\tilde f_i$ is the one for which the two-case rule is internally consistent;

(f) along the $i$-string, $\varepsilon_i(\tilde f_i^{\,n}(b_1\otimes b_2))=\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)+n$ and $\varphi_i(\tilde f_i^{\,n}(b_1\otimes b_2))=\varphi_i(b_1\otimes b_2)-n$ for $0\le n\le\varphi_i(b_1\otimes b_2)$.

No choice principle is used.

Let $\lambda\in P^+$ and let $B(\lambda)=\mathcal B(\lambda)$ be the crystal of $V(\lambda)=L(\lambda)$ from [[def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity]], which is a crystal basis by [[thm-grand-loop-existence-of-highest-weight-crystal-bases]]. Then $B(\lambda)$ is a highest weight crystal of highest weight $\lambda$ in the sense of [[def-highest-weight-element-and-connected-highest-weight-crystal]]:

(i) $b_\lambda:=v_\lambda+q\mathcal L(\lambda)$ is a highest weight element of weight $\lambda$: $wt(b_\lambda)=\lambda$ and $\tilde e_ib_\lambda=0$ for every $i\in I$;

(ii) $B(\lambda)=F(b_\lambda)$: every $b\in\mathcal B(\lambda)$ is an iterated lowering image $b=\tilde f_{i_1}\cdots\tilde f_{i_r}v_\lambda$;

(iii) $b_\lambda$ is the only highest weight element of $B(\lambda)$: $\mathcal B(\lambda)_\lambda=\{b_\lambda\}$, and for every $\mu\ne\lambda$ and every nonzero homogeneous $u\in\mathcal L(\lambda)_\mu/q\mathcal L(\lambda)_\mu$ there is $i\in I$ with $\tilde e_iu\ne0$.

No choice principle is used.

For every symmetrizable datum in the explicitly chosen label-weight category, the triples $(U_A^-,\mathcal L(\infty),\psi(\mathcal L(\infty)))$ and $(V_A(\lambda),\mathcal L(\lambda),\psi(\mathcal L(\lambda)))$ of [[def-balanced-triple-and-globalization-map]] are integrally balanced componentwise, and:

(i) their balanced $\mathbb Z$-intersections map isomorphically to $\mathbb Z\mathcal B(\infty)$ and $\mathbb Z\mathcal B(\lambda)$ respectively, and after rational extension these maps identify with the full $\mathbb Q$ crystal spaces;

(ii) the inverse lifts $G_\infty(b),G_\lambda(b)$ are bar invariant and form actual $A=\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$ bases of the divided-power modules $U_A^-$ and $V_A(\lambda)$, with finite free root and label components whose ranks equal their field dimensions (the half ranks are the classical PBW ranks);

(iii) for every $i\in I$ and $n\ge1$,
$$I_{(i,n)}^A=\bigoplus_{b\in\mathcal B(\infty)\cap\tilde f_i^n\mathcal B(\infty)}AG_\infty(b),\qquad J_{(i,n)}^A=\bigoplus_{b\in\mathcal B(\lambda)\cap\tilde f_i^n\mathcal B(\lambda)}AG_\lambda(b),$$
and in particular the module image ideal equals $V_A(\lambda)\cap F_i^nV(\lambda)$; this saturation follows from the adapted global basis and is not an initial hypothesis;

(iv) under $\pi_\lambda$, $G_\infty(b)$ maps to $0$ exactly when its crystal image is $0$ and otherwise to $G_\lambda(\pi_\lambda(b))$;

(v) if $\tilde e_ib=0$ and $n\ge1$, then $G_\infty(\tilde f_i^nb)-F_i^{(n)}G_\infty(b)\in I_{(i,n+1)}^A$. This is the exact normalized top-string transition modulo the higher string ideal, not merely a modulo-$q$ congruence.

All assertions are integral, general symmetrizable, and proved without an integral-freeness premise.

Let $\Theta=\sum_{
u\in Q_+}\Theta_
u\in\widehat C$ be the $D$-coevaluation family of the diagonal root-height completion $\widehat C$ of [[def-root-height-completion-for-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiners]], built from the normalized Drinfeld pairing $D$ of [[def-drinfeld-hopf-pairing-between-positive-and-negative-quantum-halves]] and its homogeneous dual bases ([[lem-homogeneous-dual-basis-sums-are-basis-independent-and-weightwise-finite]], [[lem-the-drinfeld-hopf-pairing-is-nondegenerate-in-each-root-degree]]). Then $\Theta$ is the unique normalized bar-coproduct intertwiner for the library coproduct $\Delta$: that is,
$$\Delta(u)\Theta=\Theta\,\overline\Delta(u)\qquad	ext{for all }u\in U_q(\mathfrak g),\qquad \overline\Delta(u)=(\psi\otimes\psi)\Delta(\psi(u)),$$
among families with $\Theta_0=1$. Its generator recursions are
$$[1\otimes E_i,\Theta_
u]=\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i}(E_i\otimes K_i)-(E_i\otimes K_i^{-1})\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i},$$
$$[F_i\otimes1,\Theta_
u]=\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i}(K_i^{-1}\otimes F_i)-(K_i\otimes F_i)\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i},$$
where components of negative root degree are $0$ and the toral parts commute because $\Theta_
u$ has toral weight zero. In rank one, $\Theta_{n\alpha_i}=(q_i-q_i^{-1})^n[n]_{q_i^2}!\,E_i^{(n)}\otimes F_i^{(n)}$ with $[n]_{q_i^2}!=\prod_{r=1}^n(1+q_i^2+\cdots+q_i^{2(r-1)})$, so that $\Theta_{\alpha_i}=(q_i-q_i^{-1})E_i\otimes F_i$. The family evaluates precisely under the explicit finite-support hypotheses of the completion. This is not the native unscaled doubled-torus $\Psi\Delta^{\mathrm{op}}$ relation, and no integral algebra coefficient claim is made.

Assume the Axiom of Choice, inherited through the crystal/global-basis and generic/classical PBW-rank suppliers. Let $U_A$ be the Lusztig divided-power integral form of [[def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form]], with its parts $U_A^-,U_A^0,U_A^+$ over $A=\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$. Then:

(i) multiplication $U_A^-\otimes_AU_A^0\otimes_AU_A^+\to U_A$ is an isomorphism;

(ii) the two divided-power halves are free $A$-modules with finite free root components of the classical PBW ranks;

(iii) the unmodified torus has the $A$-basis $K_c\prod_iK_i^{\delta_i}T_{i,0,t_i}$ with $\delta_i\in\{0,1\}$ and $c$ running over a system of representatives of $P^\vee/\sum_i\mathbb Z(d_ih_i)$; hence $U_A$ is free, and the degree-$\beta$ component is $\bigoplus_{\alpha-\gamma=\beta}U_A^-[-\gamma]\otimes_AU_A^0\otimes_AU_A^+[\alpha]$;

(iv) unique coefficients refer to products of chosen basis elements, not to arbitrary sums of simple tensors.

Let $\Theta\in\widehat C$ be the actual quasi-$R$ intertwiner of [[thm-the-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiner-exists-and-is-unique]] and let the based modules be the exact four-axiom based modules of this page with divided-power lattices. Then integral **action** is proved in the following cases:

(a) $V(\lambda)\otimes N$ for any based $N$, with the highest weight module first;

(b) $M\otimes\omega V(\lambda)$ for any based $M$, with the lowest module second, under vectorwise finite action;

(c) arbitrary $U_A$-stable based pairs in finite Cartan type;

(d) a first factor in the class $C_{\mathrm{high}}$ with arbitrary based second, or an arbitrary based first with second in $C_{\mathrm{low}}$, under vectorwise finite action, where $C_{\mathrm{high}}$ consists of exact based subquotients of weight-admissible direct sums of finite highest tensor constructions and $C_{\mathrm{low}}$ is its lowest or unsigned-Chevalley counterpart, based subobjects being saturated basis subsets and quotient maps being integral and bar compatible. The classes $C_{\mathrm{high}},C_{\mathrm{low}}$ are closed under the normalized finite tensor construction.

The full finite supported tensor-global-basis construction is proved for (a), (b), and (d) with their stated support bounds, and for case (c) whenever each raw seed has finite raw-basis correction closure in the strict transfer order (in particular for every finite-dimensional based pair), including all finite highest products and all ordered highest-prefix, arbitrary-based-middle, lowest-suffix products; direct sums require the stated upper-cone or finite-component conditions, and in the lowest case the explicit lower-cone action bound is used. No arbitrary based $O_{\mathrm{int}}$ pair is covered in general type.

In these cases $\Theta$ preserves the actual integral tensor lattice and has $A$-valued action coefficients in its tensor bases; its degree-$0$ component is the identity; every positive root component shifts the first label up and the second down, so the bar is locally weight-transfer triangular with diagonal $1$. In finite Cartan type the stronger algebra coefficients $\Theta_\nu\in U_A^+[\nu]\otimes U_A^-[-\nu]$ are also proved, and that stronger universal coefficient claim is false in general affine type. Within those supported construction classes a normalized triangular bar-correction constructs the genuine tensor global basis, so raw tensor products of lower basis vectors need not themselves be bar fixed. Case (c) asserts finite integral action and triangularity for arbitrary based finite-type pairs; these properties alone do not imply finite supported corrected lifts or a balanced tensor triple. The actual periodic rank-one example of [[lem-periodic-cyclotomic-based-sl-two-tensor-corrections-escape-every-finite-cut]] refutes that unrestricted construction assertion. The class in (d) means based subquotients of the constructed tensor bases, not arbitrary integral lattices in semisimple field modules, and the affine construction below disproves unqualified arbitrary-based action integrality while retaining the stated general symmetrizable cases.

Let $H=H_v(n)$ be the normalized type-A Hecke algebra over $A=\mathbb Z[v^{\pm1}]$ and let $a$ be the $A$-linear anti-involution with $a(v)=v$, $a(H_x)=H_{x^{-1}}$ (the reversal anti-involution of the Hecke page). Define the trace $\varepsilon:H\to A$ by $\varepsilon(H_w)=\delta_{e,w}$ on the standard basis, and the bilinear form $(-,-):H\times H\to A$ by $(h,h'):=\varepsilon(a(h)h')$. Then:

1. $\varepsilon$ and $(-,-)$ are well defined (the $H_w$ are an $A$-basis);
2. $(ph,qh')=pq\,(h,h')$ for all $p,q\in A$ and all $h,h'$;
3. $(hH_s,h')=(h,h'H_s)$ and $(H_sh,h')=(h,H_sh')$ for all $h,h'$ and every simple reflection $s$;
4. the standard basis is orthonormal, $(H_x,H_y)=\delta_{xy}$;
5. on the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis, $(\underline H_x,\underline H_y)\in v\mathbb Z[v]$ for $x\ne y$ and $(\underline H_x,\underline H_x)\in 1+v\mathbb Z[v]$;
6. the form of (4) is the standard pairing $\langle H_x,H_y\rangle=\delta_{xy}$ used in the type-A Soergel Hom formula, transported through the identification of the normalized standard bases.

All statements are integral and use no choice.

Let $\underline x=s_1\cdots s_m$ be an expression, $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)$ its real Bott-Samelson bimodule, $\mathrm{Br}_i=\chi_i\circ\varphi_i$ the Demazure operation at the $i$-th letter, and $\operatorname{Tr},\langle-,-\rangle$ the trace and intersection form of [[def-invariant-intersection-form-on-a-bott-samelson-bimodule]]. Then:

(a) as endomorphisms of $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)$ one has $\rho\cdot(-)=\sum_{i=1}^m(s_{i-1}\cdots s_1\rho)(\alpha_{s_i}^\vee)\,\chi_i\circ\varphi_i+(-)\cdot x^{-1}\rho$ (EW Lemma 3.4), where $(-)\cdot x^{-1}\rho$ denotes right multiplication by the polynomial $x^{-1}\rho$;

(b) with the rescaled forms $\langle-,-\rangle_i':=(s_{i-1}\cdots s_1\rho)(\alpha_{s_i}^\vee)\langle-,-\rangle_i$ on $\mathrm{BS}(\widehat{\underline x}_i)$ and $\langle-,-\rangle'$ their direct sum, one has $\langle b,\rho b'\rangle_{\overline{\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)}}=\langle\varphi(b),\varphi(b')\rangle'$ for all $b,b'$ in the reduced module, where $\varphi=\sum_i\varphi_i:\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)\to\bigoplus_i\mathrm{BS}(\widehat{\underline x}_i)(1)$ (EW Lemmas 6.14 and 6.15);

(c) if $\underline x$ is reduced then every scaling factor $\gamma_i:=(s_{i-1}\cdots s_1\rho)(\alpha_{s_i}^\vee)$ is strictly positive, so the rescalings change no signs in the HR statements, and the unscattered term $(-)\cdot x^{-1}\rho$ vanishes after reduction to the Soergel module;

(d) for a reduced expression, consequently left multiplication by $\rho$ on $\overline{\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)}$ factors, up to positive rescalings, as the composite $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)\to\bigoplus_i\mathrm{BS}(\widehat{\underline x}_i)(1)\to\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)(2)$, and this composite is the first differential of the Rouquier complex $F_{s_1}\cdots F_{s_m}$ followed by its dual map, up to the same positive rescalings.

Assume $\mathrm{S}(x)$, i.e. $\operatorname{ch}(B_x^R)=\underline H_x$. Then:

1. the space of invariant forms on $B_x^R$ is one dimensional over $\mathbb R$, and every non-zero invariant form is non-degenerate;
2. if $B_x^R$ is embedded as a direct summand of a Bott-Samelson bimodule $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)$ for a reduced expression of $x$, the restriction of the intersection form is a positive multiple of the intersection form on $B_x^R$;
3. consequently $\mathrm{HR}(x)$ holds for the intrinsic intersection form if and only if $\mathrm{HRbar}(x)$ holds for every (equivalently: some) embedding, and the two normalizations differ by a positive scalar, so no sign changes;
4. if $B=\bigoplus_zB_z^{R,m_z}$ with $\mathrm{S}(z)$ for all $z$, then non-degenerate symmetric invariant forms on $B$ are direct sums of the forms on the isotypic components tensored with nonsingular real symmetric multiplicity-space pairings, and an orthogonal decomposition into isotypic components can always be chosen.

Assume $\mathrm S(y)$ for all $y\le x$, and let $F_x$ be a minimal Rouquier complex of a reduced expression of $x$. Then:

(1) $^0F_x\cong B_x^R$;

(2) for every $i\ge1$, $^iF_x\cong\bigoplus_{z<x}B_z^R(i)^{m_{z,i}}$ with $m_{z,i}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$, so that $F_x$ lies in $pK^b_{\le0}\cap pK^b_{\ge0}$ and is linear: every indecomposable summand of $^iF_x$ carries the internal shift exactly $i$;

(3) the multiplicities satisfy the parity vanishing $m_{z,i}=0$ unless $i\equiv\ell(x)-\ell(z)\pmod2$, and consequently, writing the standard basis element as $H_x=\sum_{z\le x}g_{z,x}\underline H_z$ (the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients), one has $g_{x,x}=1$, $g_{z,x}=\sum_i(-1)^im_{z,i}v^i$, and $(-1)^{\ell(x)-\ell(z)}g_{z,x}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}[v^{\pm1}]$.

The statement is conditional exactly on $\mathrm S(y)$ for $y\le x$; it uses neither $\mathrm S(x')$ for $x'>x$ nor the final character theorem, and it is the induction step of the simultaneous induction.

Fix $x,s$ with $xs>x$ and assume $\mathrm{S}(y)$ and $\mathrm{HR}(y)$ for all $y<xs$; fix embeddings and the induced forms, a reduced expression $\underline x$ of $x$ and a generator $c_{\mathrm{bot}}$ of the one-dimensional space $\overline{B_y^R}^{-\ell(y)}$. Then:

1. the evaluation map $\mathrm{ev}:\operatorname{Hom}^0(B_y^R,M)\to\overline M^{-\ell(y)}$, $f\mapsto\overline f(c_{\mathrm{bot}})$, where $M=B_x^RB_s^R$ and $\overline f$ is the induced map on right reductions, is injective with image contained in the primitive subspace $P^{-\ell(y)}_\rho=\ker(\rho^{\ell(y)+1})$;
2. $\mathrm{ev}$ is an isometry up to the positive factor $N=\langle\rho^{\ell(y)}c_{\mathrm{bot}},c_{\mathrm{bot}}\rangle_{\overline{B_y^R}}>0$: $N(f,g)^{x,s}_y=(\mathrm{ev}(f),\mathrm{ev}(g))^{-\ell(y)}_\rho$ for all $f,g$;
3. consequently $\mathrm{HR}(x,s)$ implies $\mathrm{S}(xs)$: a definite form restricted to a subspace stays non-degenerate, so the local forms are non-degenerate and the detection theorem applies.

Write $\underline H_w=\sum_{y\le w}h_{y,w}H_y$ for the expansion of the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis element in the standard basis and $\underline H_x\underline H_y=\sum_z\mu^z_{x,y}\underline H_z$ for the structure constants of the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis. Then:

(1) $h_{y,w}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}[v]$ for all $y\le w$; equivalently the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials $P_{y,w}(q)=v^{-(\ell(w)-\ell(y))}h_{y,w}(v)$ with $q=v^{-2}$ have nonnegative integer coefficients, and in particular the $\mu$-coefficients $\mu(y,w)=[v]h_{y,w}$ are nonnegative;

(2) $\mu^z_{x,y}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}[v^{\pm1}]$: the structure constants are nonnegative, and $\mu^z_{x,y}$ is the character of the multiplicity space of $B_z^R$ in the tensor product $B_x^R\otimes_SB_y^R$, i.e. its graded multiplicity;

(3) the expansion of the product $\operatorname{ch}(B_x)\operatorname{ch}(B_y)$ in the standard basis has nonnegative coefficients, namely the $\Delta$-multiplicities of $B_xB_y$.

No positivity of Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials is assumed anywhere; the corollary is the local proof of the positivity clause from the character theorem.

Let $G,H$ be finite groups. For finite-dimensional complex representations $V$ of $G$ and $W$ of $H$, their external tensor product is the representation of $G\times H$ on $V\otimes_{\mathbb C}W$ with $(g,h)\cdot(v\otimes w):=gv\otimes hw$. Its character satisfies $\chi_{V\boxtimes W}(g,h)=\chi_V(g)\chi_W(h)$ ([[def-external-direct-product-of-groups]], [[thm-external-direct-product-is-a-group]], [[def-tensor-product-of-complex-representations]], [[thm-characters-of-direct-sums-tensor-products-and-duals]], [[def-character-of-a-complex-representation]]). Extend this operation $\mathbb Z$-bilinearly from irreducible characters to $R(G)\times R(H)$, using their irreducible-character bases ([[def-virtual-character-and-character-ring-of-a-finite-group]], [[thm-irreducible-complex-characters-form-an-orthonormal-basis-of-the-class-functions]]). Then:

(i) the induced map $R(G)\otimes_{\mathbb Z}R(H)\to R(G\times H)$, $\chi\otimes\psi\mapsto\chi\boxtimes\psi$, is an isomorphism of rings, carries $1\otimes1$ to the trivial character, and satisfies $(\chi\boxtimes\psi)(\chi'\boxtimes\psi')=(\chi\chi')\boxtimes(\psi\psi')$ for all $\chi,\chi'\in R(G)$, $\psi,\psi'\in R(H)$;

(ii) as $\chi$ and $\psi$ range over the irreducible characters, the elements $\chi\boxtimes\psi$ are pairwise distinct and form an orthonormal $\mathbb Z$-basis of $R(G\times H)$; in particular every honest character of $G\times H$ is a nonnegative integral combination of the $\chi\boxtimes\psi$. No choice principle is used.

Let $f\in R(S_m)$, $g\in R(S_n)$, $h\in R(S_r)$, and let $\circ$ be the outer induction product of [[def-outer-induction-product-for-symmetric-group-characters]] on the graded abelian group $R_S=\bigoplus_{n\ge0}R(S_n)$ ([[def-graded-ordinary-representation-ring-of-symmetric-groups]]). Then:

(i) $f\circ g\in R(S_{m+n})$, and $\circ$ is $\mathbb Z$-bilinear and distributive over addition;

(ii) associativity: $(f\circ g)\circ h=f\circ(g\circ h)$ in $R(S_{m+n+r})$;

(iii) commutativity: $f\circ g=g\circ f$ in $R(S_{m+n})$;

(iv) unitality: for the trivial character $e$ of $S_0$, $e\circ f=f=f\circ e$.

Consequently $R_S$ is a commutative graded $\mathbb Z$-algebra with unit $e$ and degree-$n$ part $R(S_n)$. No choice principle is used.

Let $R_S=\bigoplus_{n\ge0}R(S_n)$ be the graded commutative ring of [[thm-outer-induction-makes-the-graded-representation-group-a-commutative-ring]], with outer product $\circ$, unit the trivial character $e$ of $S_0$ (so $R(S_0)=\mathbb Z\cdot1$), and let $\Delta$ be the restriction coproduct of [[def-restriction-coproduct-on-the-graded-symmetric-group-character-ring]]. Let $\varepsilon:R_S\to\mathbb Z$ be the augmentation defined by $\varepsilon(f)=f(1)$ on $R(S_0)$ and $\varepsilon|_{R(S_n)}=0$ for $n\ge1$ ([[def-graded-ordinary-representation-ring-of-symmetric-groups]], [[def-virtual-character-and-character-ring-of-a-finite-group]]). Then:

(i) $(R_S,\circ,e,\Delta,\varepsilon)$ is a connected graded bialgebra over $\mathbb Z$ ([[def-graded-bialgebra-and-hopf-algebra]]): $\Delta$ and $\varepsilon$ are $\mathbb Z$-algebra homomorphisms and satisfy coassociativity and the counit identities;

(ii) by [[lem-connected-graded-bialgebra-has-a-recursive-antipode]], $R_S$ has a unique antipode and is a graded Hopf algebra, with the antipode computed by the reduced-coproduct recursion;

(iii) the Frobenius characteristic is an isomorphism of graded Hopf algebras from $R_S$ onto $\Lambda$ equipped with the diagonal coproduct $\Delta_\Lambda f=f(x,y)$, multiplication of symmetric functions, counit the constant term, and antipode $S(f)=(-1)^n\omega(f)$ for $f\in\Lambda^n$. No choice principle is used.

Let $F$ be a field, $q\in F^{	imes}$, $d,n\ge1$, and let $S=S(d,n)$ be the q-Schur algebra with Weyl modules $W^{\lambda}$ for $\lambda\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$ ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional right $S$-module and let $(a_{\lambda})_{\lambda\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)}$ be integers such that $$\dim_F igl(M\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}} igr)=\sum_{\lambda\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)}a_{\lambda}\,\dim_F igl(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}} igr)$$ for every partition $
u\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$, where $\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}}$ are the diagonal semistandard basis elements ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). Then $[M]=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}[W^{\lambda}]$ in the Grothendieck group $G_0(S	ext{-mod})$ ([[def-grothendieck-group-of-an-essentially-small-abelian-category]]). Consequently equality of all semistandard weight-space dimensions forces equality in the Grothendieck group.

